# A clone of someone else's engine fork, sitting inside ours.
#
# This directory has its OWN .git. `git add` on it does not add 1439 files, it
# adds a GITLINK - a pointer to a commit in a repository this one does not own and
# cannot fetch - which is worse than either committing the tree or ignoring it:
# a fresh clone gets an empty directory and a submodule nobody declared.
#
# It is not ours to track. 33 MB of a third-party Perfect Dark fork with its own
# LICENSE and provenance, never tracked in this repository's history, and the
# working copy here is scratch space. Ignoring it costs nothing and removes the
# one way `git add -A` can quietly corrupt the index.
/NitrousAndCodexPerfectDarkKAI/

# The documentation tier: `docs/` is public, `docs/private/` is not.
#
# Public is what a contributor needs to build, run, or report a bug, and it is
# tracked normally - this rule needs no `git add -f`. Private is the
# engineering record (audits, design notes, session handoffs, PR write-ups),
# which stays on disk here and lives in the private engineering repo. The test
# for which is which is written down in CONTRIBUTING.md and enforced by
# scripts/port/check_docs_tier.sh.
#
# The old rule ignored the whole tree and allow-listed one file, while ten
# others were force-added past it, so the written policy and the index
# disagreed. A rule that needs -f to obey is not a rule.
/docs/private/

# Screenshots and other loose images at the repository root are working files,
# not documentation: the Discord captures and window shots that get dropped here
# while investigating something. Ignoring the pattern rather than the names means
# the next one is covered too. Root only - port/include/presskit and
# port/include/icon.png are tracked and must stay tracked.
/*.png

# boot_flag_test.sh capture folders (dated; they hold 2 MB BMPs per shot)
/captures/
